 lampedusa this has reached a  point where i think things just cannot  go on as there are but i do not pretend  i have a solution there are many small  solutions at on at the edges of the  problem but not at the center michel  michelle says he doesn't have the  solution do you perhaps have one laura  lukini well of course i mean the the  problem with the with the illegal  immigrants that stays in italy has to do  with this immigration law that we have  because  those immigrants are  being pressing press charge for illegal  immigration if they  don't have documents with them they can  stay in italy in any legal way there's  no way to to even if they find a job but  there's no way to to change their status  they will be illegal forever there's no  way that an employee with a good will  can could change the status of these  people that put a lot of pressure on  employees and that put a lot of power to  mafia or people exploiting this  situation so that that's why there are a  huge uh organi  there are a huge organization that  they're exploiting these these people  for to sell uh illegal uh illegal stuff  sometimes because those banks the the  fake specs that they're selling in on  the street of italian cities are illegal  as well and uh  using these people for example i don't  know in tomatoes uh  uh camps in in southern italy uh  under the constant threat of being  denounced to the police so that that's a  problem that we have with this law that  there has been the instrument at least  to change the status of these people but  of course this problem is not just an  italian problem quite on the country  it's a european problem let's touch upon  the issue which has come up a few times  here namely the issue of trafficking uh  the smugglers uh that are profiting if  you will from  from the the uh  the the the bad fortune uh of these  people coming uh to europe or trying to  come to europe there is an argument that  they're saying closing the borders will  give even more powers  to the traffickers it will not solve the  problem quite on the concrete will  exacerbate the problem would you agree  well the entire  trafficking business is just a reaction  on the frontex policy as long as the  border is totally sealed off there will  be traffickers if there would be no  traffickers people would die anyway  because they would try to organize the  boats on their own they wouldn't stop in  north africa they would try to get boats  to set over to the  italian shores to the spanish coast and  the situation would be the same so it's  not the human traffickers who are to  blame for what is happening for example  in lampedusa it is the fact that there  is simply no way to legally enter europe  hans peter friedrich once more the  german interior minister said something  in the context of this tragedy he says  as much as we regret the casualties of  course it needs to be stated and made  clear that most of these asylum seekers  generally speaking are economic migrants  not political refugees  would you  does does do the numbers and you know  this issue quite well do the numbers  back up his statement well it is true  that not everybody who who comes over to  italy in a boat is a political refugee  and there are labor  migrants there is a labor migration it  has been it has been there always and it  will be like this in the future as well  but we should stop to regard this as a  problem uh  migration  research and also  a  macroeconomists are claiming that europe  and germany needs immigration and it  does not only need immigration from  within the european union but also from  outside there are different numbers on  this but even a  commission that has been installed in  the late 1990s by the party of minister  friedrich cdu has has tried to to  somehow evaluate how high the necessity  of immigration for the european economy  is for the german economy is  and they said that at least one hundred  thousand people should move in every  year in order to satisfy the necessity  of the economy and to avoid that the  european society shrinks  how helpful is this distinction between  economic migrants and political refugees  uh at the end of the day they're all on  the boat they're all trying to come here  and they are all uh if you will taking  the risks to get here so what does it  matter at the end of the day why they're  coming now economic migrants  are not  necessary they  perhaps they have a chance  to adapt and make a useful contribution  in the countries of arrival  even where they are at first  in the first place are not very welcome  i do not believe that  a hundred thousand refugees from syria  or from eritrea  can really  be the answer to the problem  uh that we are facing in terms of  european demographies because it's more  or less the same everywhere  you have to  i mean  bringing in  refugees who in  take the people from syria probably 99  of them would love to stay in syria  which is a beautiful country very  civilized uh  great cultural benefits but  the situation being what it is syria is  falling apart hundreds of thousands of  people are fleeing tens of thousands are  dead  they have never wanted they never wanted  to settle down in europe  and their qualifications to make a  decent living  are  in many cases good in even more cases  probably very bad  this is not  this this spontaneous  uncontrollable migration is not the  answer to what we really need in the  medium and long term and let's look very  briefly the last round because we are  running unfortunately out of time what  is it that we need what what needs to be  done here in the light of this tragedy  give us a couple of concrete factors a  combination of factors that are working  on our side and then working on the  other side  that includes development aid but it  also includes policing it includes  explaining and enlightening people  people have overblown ideas of the  paradise the land of milk and honey that  awaits them on the northern shores of  the mediterranean  laura lukini what what what is your  proposal here i think we should  establish uh force aid forces in the  mediterranean  from the european side and they told so  should be organized information centers  or place where the people could the  zealand asylum seeker could go to to get  information and to to travel safer there  are two things that could be done in a  short term  christian  we've talked about the pros and cons  here on the show extensively last word  uh goes to you what does the eu need to  do now your the european union needs to  stop to regard migration as a threat it  needs to um allow refugees to get in  legally without risking their lives and  dying in the mediterranean sea while  trying to get to europe and the european  union needs to start to set up a system  that equally and fairly distributes the  burdens of asylum  amongst other member states and do you  think that will happen realistically  speaking i'm afraid it is not so likely  and  so so a rather pessimistic tone than if  i understand you correct we have seen in  the last 20 years that the policy has  always been towards the the  prevention of immigration and i'm afraid  that just because this time 300 have 300  people have been dying in one day  this is not going to change so easily  well the eu ministers eu interior  ministers in luxembourg could not come  to an agreement and the  eu asylum policies are still not unified  and common and i'm afraid we too on the  show could not find uh common ground but  i think we've highlighted very well the  issues that are at stake here and we  will of course hear equadriga continue  to follow the story very closely i want  to thank my guests  for their opinions their insights into  this very intricate and complex not  black and white issue i want to thank  you out there for tuning in and looking  forward to seeing you again next week  for a new edition of quadriga  you
 

